@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — the waiver that runs in both directions
#
# One generalization, formed from four attested claims in three transmissions
# between which no route has been proposed, and bounded by two cases the
# counter-search found in the holding before the node was written.
#
# It is filed on its own and not merged into what-attainment-relieves, whose
# statement it extends and whose antecedent it does not meet. That one is about
# a transmission letting somebody out because he has attained. This one is
# about the same requirement being let go at the other end as well, for the
# practitioner who cannot meet it — and about the requirement that is let go
# for neither, which is the part that does the work.
#
# Why the pair matters more than either half. A transmission that waives a
# condition only upward could be read as ranking its people; one that waives
# the same condition downward as well is saying something about the condition:
# that it belongs to the manner of the operation and not to its working. And
# where the same transmission refuses both waivers for a second condition, it
# has sorted its own requirements into two classes and told a reader which one
# it thinks carries the effect.
#

pmc:TheWaiverRunsBothWays a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "The outward requirement is waived at both ends, and the requirement on body and conduct at neither"@en, "Die äußere Forderung wird an beiden Enden erlassen, die an Körper und Verhalten an keinem"@de ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where an operative transmission sets a requirement that is fixed outside the practitioner — a date, an hour, a place built for the purpose, a rarity of material — and then releases anyone from it, it releases at both ends: the practitioner who has attained and the practitioner who cannot meet it, the second by being given a second route to the same claimed result. Where the same transmission sets a requirement bearing on the practitioner's body or conduct, it releases nobody from it at either end, and that is the requirement it names when it accounts for a user in whom nothing happened."@en ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:AyurvedaKutyadhikarin, pmc:AyurvedaDosatyagapurvakatva, pmc:ClaviculaAdeptiExempti, pmc:ShouyiMianLiri ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "The witnesses, and the Indian one carries both halves at once, four sections apart in a single lesson. The rasayana lesson of the Caraka builds a chamber with thick walls, small openings and two outer rings of rooms, and binds the entry into it to the sun's northern course, the lighted fortnight, a propitious lunation and constellation and a favourable muhurta and karana. It then says that for those endued with strength, free from disease, intellectual, self-controlled, forgiving and having men and riches at their command, for them only is the ordinance about entering a hut beneficial; unto those of a different disposition the ordinance does not apply, and they take the rasayana exposed to sun and wind instead. The same section offers a third preparation expressly to those who seek ease and are unable to seek out herbs and undergo the discomforts of administration. The chamber and its calendar are therefore waived for the majority of users, and the result claimed is not reduced."@en ;
    skos:note "The other half of the same lesson is the second witness and it is stated in the next verses. That person who, without casting off at least the graver faults of both body and mind, uses rasayana never reaps the effects of rasayana; and rasayanic combinations succeed with those who are cleansed in respect of mind and body and who have their souls under control. Nobody is released from that, and it is the only thing in the lesson that accounts for a user in whom nothing happened. The lesson is willing to call its chamber optional and is not willing to call its conduct optional."@en ;
    skos:note "The upward half comes from two transmissions that know nothing of each other or of the first. The Key of Solomon says that so exact a preparation of days and hours is not necessary for those who are adepts in the art but is extremely necessary for apprentices and beginners, and a search of both its books found no corresponding relief from the bath, the fast, the continence or the confession for anybody. The eighteenth chapter of the Baopuzi says that one who can guard the One need not divine the day or choose the hour, no longer consults the siting calendars and does not avoid the year star or the taboos of his year and fate — while the eighth chapter of the same book says that the circulation of the qi is of no benefit in the hours of dead qi, so that the same author sets the condition and grants the exemption from it."@en ;
    skos:note "What the four together show and what they do not. Three transmissions, no proposed route between any two of them, and in each the thing released is a time or a place and in each the thing not released is a state of the practitioner. That is the whole of the claim. It says nothing about whether either kind of requirement does anything; a transmission may be waiving the date because it never mattered or because the adept has grown past it, and this statement does not choose."@en ;
    skos:note "The counter-search was carried against the compiled holding before this was written, and it was put as two questions. First, whether any transmission here waives a bodily or conduct requirement for a class of practitioner — the search that would break the second clause. Second, whether any transmission, faced with a practitioner who cannot meet its outward requirement, gives him no second route but removes him. Both returned something and neither refutes."@en ;
    skos:note "To the first, the nearest cases are the ones the earlier generalization already records and one more. The Zhen'gao raises the question explicitly about a bodily condition and answers no: even a person entered in the jade registers is not thereby exempt from the loss that frequent emission causes. The Shangqing term of eighteen years attaches no condition that would exempt a practitioner of that standing who still casts a shadow at noon. No passage found waives a fast, a washing, a term of continence or a dietary rule for anyone."@en ;
    skos:note "To the second, one clear case and it bounds the first clause. The Tungus material reports that a shaman who is old and physically weak cannot carry out very long and difficult performances, and that a shaman may be excused from attending only if she is female and pregnant or otherwise under a prohibition; without such an excuse he may lose his position. There the deficient practitioner gets no lighter route — he loses the office. The difference from the three witnesses is that the requirement he fails is not an outward one but his own strength, so the antecedent of the statement is not met; but the case shows what the alternative looks like and it is why the statement is worded about outward requirements and not about requirements in general."@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by an operative transmission that sets an outward requirement, releases its adepts from it, and gives the practitioner who cannot meet it no second route to the same result but exclusion; or by one that waives a bodily or conduct requirement — a fast, a washing, a term of continence, a rule of diet or of anger — for either class, at either end. Either of those breaks it, and the second would break the earlier statement with it."@en ;
    skos:note "Weakened, without being refuted, if the three transmissions turn out to be doing the same thing for unrelated reasons, which is the likeliest way this fails. A medical handbook that offers an easier route is selling to more people; a grimoire that exempts its adepts is flattering them; a chapter arguing that one method replaces several thousand others has an interest in cancelling the others' rules. Three different motives could produce one pattern, and the pattern would then be a fact about who writes operative literature and not about what the literature holds. The statement is examinable either way: a fourth and fifth transmission with the same asymmetry and different motives would tell against the coincidence."@en ;
    skos:note "The count is four claims in three transmissions and one of the three supplies two of the four. Read as a spread that is three and not four, and the statement is worded as a recurring shape and not as a law."@en .
